WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:05.640
Jim, do you have an early
story of an experience

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:07.890
when you were first
starting out of having

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.920
to make sure that a
workplace was accessible?

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:12.150
I've had many.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:16.020
I was born with spina
bifida, and I grew up

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.130
at a split-level house.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.740
My parents never moved.

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.358
And I have really good
upper-body strength.

00:00:23.358 --> 00:00:25.650
So I used to climb up and
down the stairs all the time,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.050
and I did that until
I went off to college.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.310
The wheelchair
was in the garage.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.530
Which really prepared me
from entering, as Kaitlyn

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:36.750
and I also talk about, this
world that wasn't built for us.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.150
And that is certainly
true in the theater.

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:41.850
For the summer theater
job that I wanted to get,

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.290
the booth was inaccessible.

00:00:44.290 --> 00:00:46.230
It was up a flight of stairs.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:51.252
And so I went down to Santa
Maria in central California,

00:00:51.252 --> 00:00:52.710
and I climbed up
a flight of stairs

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.600
for them, for them to
see that I could do it.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.180
And my solution that
whole summer was that --

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.770
I do have two wheelchairs.

00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:01.290
If you're fortunate,
you have a backup chair,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:02.880
and I had two push chairs.

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.430
And so I had one
left up at the top,

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.780
and I would just climb
up and down to get there.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.410
I did a sound design for a
musical at the Public Theater

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:20.080
back in 1982, and I'd worked
with the director before,

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.380
and he wanted me there.

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.850
But I spent about an hour trying
to convince the production

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:30.390
manager that, physically,
I could do the job.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:32.550
And indeed, at the
time, the Public Theater

00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.360
was not accessible.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.140
So I had to pull my
wheelchair up the steps

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.300
into the building every day.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:43.680
I would also like to go back to
your example of being an intern

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.210
and bringing coffee, right?

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.610
So one of my first
days supervising on

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:49.770
set on a major
motion picture, that

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.810
was the job that I was given.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:54.690
And I quickly
thought on the spot

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.180
of getting a crate
for the coffee.

00:01:57.180 --> 00:01:59.950
I was responsible for getting
60 coffees during break

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.360
and getting it back there.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:04.340
And luckily, we were shooting
at my alma mater, USC,

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.680
where I was very familiar
with the terrain, not

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.140
having a car of my own, and I
kind of just rolled everywhere.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.620
And so I immediately called
up my barista, who knows me

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.990
by name, saying, hey,
so sorry, but can I

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:18.273
please put in this large order?

00:02:18.273 --> 00:02:20.190
And they're like, oh,
yeah, no problem, right?

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.260
So I got the 60 coffees.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.560
I got a crate, put all
the coffees in there,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.240
called up another good friend
who has a coffee cart, brought

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:28.060
everything over.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:30.960
So essentially, I was
able to complete that task

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:32.580
within about 15 minutes.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.350
And I think this is
really just showing

00:02:34.350 --> 00:02:37.530
we're just so used to
thinking outside the box,

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:39.690
because the world has
not been designed for us.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:42.630
And we just have to do
that on a daily basis.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.700
My supervisor at the
time was so impressed

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.220
that I was able to do that
that then he had to figure out

00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:50.220
what other tasks to give me
to fill up my time that day,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.610
and I was finally able to
do some VFX work, which

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.860
is kind of the whole
point of paying your dues

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.470
and bringing coffees and
getting the meals right,

00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.730
and all that jazz.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.290
In 2016, a friend of mine,
comedian, actor, disability

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.490
rights advocate, Danny Woodburn,
told me about a situation

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.920
that he became aware
of in New York.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.200
There was a bill
that the state was

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.280
doing for writers in
television, television writers

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.610
and directors, to be more
inclusive, so that their tax

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:24.600
incentives would
include minority groups.

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:27.690
The problem Danny had with
it, and we all have with it,

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.200
is that it didn't
include disability.

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.650
And when he brought it up,
it wasn't ever addressed.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.240
The law did pass.

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.130
And I think there's
something similar that

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.070
is in the works in California.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:45.030
And as we all know, tax
incentives are a big deal.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:47.280
As a producer--
that's why we shoot

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.870
in Georgia and other places
that have tax incentives.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.850
This is kind of a
new issue that could

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:57.390
be really beneficial
for hiring of minorities

00:03:57.390 --> 00:03:58.990
behind the camera.

00:03:58.990 --> 00:03:59.490
Yes.

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.390
So for visual effects,
kind of like other aspects

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:07.230
of filmmaking, we're constantly
chasing tax incentives as well.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.780
From my understanding,
any incentive

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.640
that comes from a
state should follow

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.019
state rules of hiring people
on a diverse spectrum.

00:04:16.019 --> 00:04:19.360
And that, it does
include disability.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.770
And I think tax incentives,
although it might be some --

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:24.960
you know, on the very top
of some people's lists.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.560
But I think it just can be
added into a very long list

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.110
of benefits of hiring diverse
artists and diverse vendors.

00:04:33.110 --> 00:04:34.950
For my company,
Alpha Studios, we

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.560
have been certified
as a disabled business

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:39.900
enterprise and
women-owned enterprise,

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:42.150
and we're in the
process of getting

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:46.450
the minority-owned business
enterprise as well.

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:48.720
And I was so glad to hear--

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.520
with all the chatters and
all the conversations that

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:56.040
we're having, and also just
riding the tides of this

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.180
moment, you know, with
Black Lives Matter --

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:01.920
that the minority certification,
they are busier than ever,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.270
because they want to get ahead
of the wave of having minority

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:08.630
business certified,
so we can be in front

00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:10.560
of the line for
these tax incentives.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.020
I think that's just
one of many things

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:17.580
that are all slowly
helping us moving

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.840
towards a similar goal, which
is, there's lots of benefits

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.480
of hiring people that you might
not have worked with before.

